00:01:52.005 --> 00:01:53.862
Clear Testable Acceptance Criteria.

00:01:54.469 --> 00:01:57.230
Our presenter for this session
is Russ Weakley from Australia.

00:01:57.966 --> 00:02:01.462
Russ has over 30 years of experience
in design and development as a user

00:02:01.462 --> 00:02:05.135
experience professional, front-end
developer, author, and trainer.

00:02:05.751 --> 00:02:09.324
He has a deep focus on accessibility
and inclusion, and has spent much of

00:02:09.324 --> 00:02:13.354
his career helping organizations build
digital experiences that are usable and

00:02:13.355 --> 00:02:15.501
inclusive for people of all abilities.

00:02:16.235 --> 00:02:19.534
Russ has extensive experience planning
and conducting user research and

00:02:19.534 --> 00:02:23.632
usability testing with people with
disabilities, helping organizations

00:02:23.655 --> 00:02:27.259
gain direct insight into the barriers
and opportunities experienced by

00:02:27.260 --> 00:02:29.103
people with diverse access needs.

00:02:29.799 --> 00:02:33.377
Please feel free to add your questions
in the Zoom Q&A section, and we'll

00:02:33.378 --> 00:02:34.817
answer them at the end of the session.

00:02:35.439 --> 00:02:38.097
You can use the chat function
to connect with other attendees.

00:02:38.834 --> 00:02:40.275
And with that, let's begin.

00:02:41.330 --> 00:02:41.846
Hey, everyone.

00:02:41.899 --> 00:02:43.325
Welcome to my session.

00:02:43.326 --> 00:02:47.376
My name's Russ Weakley, and I'm gonna
be talking about making accessibility

00:02:47.376 --> 00:02:51.645
actionable, and we're looking at
clear testable acceptance criteria.

00:02:52.847 --> 00:02:54.225
So let's start with an introduction.

00:02:54.440 --> 00:02:55.328
What's the problem?

00:02:55.853 --> 00:02:59.600
So I do a lot of training and working
with teams across different countries

00:02:59.618 --> 00:03:03.326
and different organizations, and there's
one common problem that I see a lot.

00:03:04.042 --> 00:03:09.116
And that is that teams often tack on
statements to their definition of done,

00:03:09.450 --> 00:03:15.144
and it's something like, "Must be WCAG
2.2 compliant," this incredibly broad

00:03:15.144 --> 00:03:17.469
statement, um, just tacked on at the end.

00:03:17.902 --> 00:03:22.829
And it's too broad and too vague, uh,
to test in, uh, any sort of criteria.

00:03:23.255 --> 00:03:25.435
Uh, it's the opposite of good practice.

00:03:25.996 --> 00:03:28.173
Um, so what is this session about then?

00:03:28.194 --> 00:03:31.519
Well, I'm gonna t- uh, walk
you through how to write

00:03:31.844 --> 00:03:36.510
clear, precise, and meaningful
acceptance criteria, particularly

00:03:36.708 --> 00:03:39.060
accessibility acceptance criteria.

00:03:39.786 --> 00:03:43.950
And these criteria are designed
so they can work on pages,

00:03:44.194 --> 00:03:46.216
uh, flows, and components.

00:03:47.656 --> 00:03:49.676
So I'm just gonna start at the
very beginning for some people

00:03:49.676 --> 00:03:50.620
that may not be aware of it.

00:03:51.096 --> 00:03:52.800
What are acceptance criteria?

00:03:52.826 --> 00:03:54.526
Forgetting about the accessibility ones.

00:03:55.155 --> 00:04:00.489
So they're basically clear, specificul,
sp- specific testable conditions

00:04:00.855 --> 00:04:05.827
that any sort of product you build or
user story, et cetera, um, in order

00:04:05.828 --> 00:04:10.005
to determine that they're complete,
or in Sprint te- uh, terminology,

00:04:10.237 --> 00:04:13.718
they're done And what do they do?

00:04:14.110 --> 00:04:16.382
Well, they give us a lot of things.

00:04:16.392 --> 00:04:19.840
So the first one is that we all have a
shared understanding of what's going on.

00:04:21.450 --> 00:04:26.676
They define what done is, as I said, and
they set, set the scope very clearly.

00:04:26.869 --> 00:04:29.674
If you have something broad
like must be WCAG 2., 2.2

00:04:29.675 --> 00:04:31.817
compliant, it's incredibly broad.

00:04:32.001 --> 00:04:35.541
What we want is to have f-
very clear, very defined scope.

00:04:36.467 --> 00:04:37.794
They help guide the team.

00:04:39.544 --> 00:04:42.221
And they, if you're done
well, support testing.

00:04:42.585 --> 00:04:46.194
A lot of the discuss- discussions I'm
gonna be s- going through today will not

00:04:46.194 --> 00:04:51.273
only help teams, but will be really useful
for QAs who are at the other end of this,

00:04:51.493 --> 00:04:55.073
because they're the poor people that
have to test against whatever you write.

00:04:55.329 --> 00:04:59.407
So our job is to make it easier for
developers and designers, but more

00:04:59.408 --> 00:05:01.567
importantly for QAs at the other end.

00:05:02.704 --> 00:05:04.857
So let's talk about some
key characteristics of

00:05:04.857 --> 00:05:07.059
good acceptance criteria.

00:05:08.372 --> 00:05:10.608
We've said this a couple of times,
but they've gotta be clear and

00:05:10.608 --> 00:05:13.060
concise and understandable by anyone.

00:05:13.220 --> 00:05:14.852
They should be unambiguous.

00:05:16.335 --> 00:05:20.831
The most important point we're gonna talk
about throughout these criteria, a lot

00:05:20.831 --> 00:05:22.156
of people write these vague statements.

00:05:22.480 --> 00:05:24.977
They should be 100% testable.

00:05:25.301 --> 00:05:29.261
That means that a QA who's not even
knowledgeable about accessibility

00:05:29.440 --> 00:05:33.365
should be able to get that criteria
and understand what they have to do.

00:05:33.693 --> 00:05:37.050
And ideally, the way we write
them in this session, every single

00:05:37.052 --> 00:05:39.171
statement should be a pass or a fail.

00:05:40.547 --> 00:05:41.710
They should be user-focused.

00:05:41.765 --> 00:05:45.546
They should be working not on some
abstract concept, but especially

00:05:45.550 --> 00:05:49.557
for acce-- uh, uh, accessibility
ones, they should be user-focused.

00:05:50.774 --> 00:05:52.258
And they should be very specific.

00:05:52.529 --> 00:05:55.709
A lot of people bundle these broad
concepts together, but you'll see that

00:05:55.709 --> 00:05:57.672
when we talk about it, it's one-to-one.

00:05:59.094 --> 00:06:02.796
So that's just general acceptance
criteria, but what about

00:06:02.812 --> 00:06:04.545
accessibility versions of that?

00:06:04.918 --> 00:06:08.875
So these are just a subset of
the broader acceptance criteria.

00:06:09.451 --> 00:06:14.689
But the one difference is they define
accessibility requirements along the same

00:06:14.689 --> 00:06:20.121
lines so that that product, when it's
done, it's also considered accessible.

00:06:21.851 --> 00:06:25.109
So where can all these accessibility
acceptance criteria be used?

00:06:25.455 --> 00:06:28.310
Well, as we said, the most
important place that I see them

00:06:28.315 --> 00:06:30.085
used is definition of done.

00:06:30.287 --> 00:06:33.999
That is the, the home for these
acceptance criteria, and they allow

00:06:34.004 --> 00:06:38.707
to confirm whether, as we said before,
that thing is accessible or complete.

00:06:39.461 --> 00:06:43.316
They can also be added to user stories
a slightly level before, so they

00:06:43.316 --> 00:06:48.274
help dictate how that thing, um, is
gonna work before it even begins.

00:06:49.182 --> 00:06:53.485
They can be moved back in the process
to design requirements, so you can write

00:06:53.485 --> 00:06:58.082
acceptance criteria that designers do as
part of their checks before, you know,

00:06:58.098 --> 00:07:01.929
th-they, they consider it complete and
before they hand over to developers.

00:07:03.278 --> 00:07:07.125
Really important for developers as well
when they're building products, um,

00:07:07.349 --> 00:07:11.149
either way, way back, um, when they're
building design system components

00:07:11.415 --> 00:07:13.302
or during sprint cycles, et cetera.

00:07:13.584 --> 00:07:17.378
Um, they provide developers with clear
guidelines on how they've gotta be built.

00:07:18.453 --> 00:07:22.377
And as I said before, the most
important people are QAs and testers,

00:07:22.655 --> 00:07:24.583
that it helps support them as well.

00:07:25.544 --> 00:07:29.512
But a few places that they, they use
that I find them really valuable sit

00:07:29.534 --> 00:07:34.466
beyond that normal sprint cycle, and
the first one is I grew up in a world of

00:07:34.466 --> 00:07:39.548
design systems, and, uh, they're really
valuable in the design system world.

00:07:39.819 --> 00:07:45.237
So you can help, um, use acceptance
criteria to define exactly how components

00:07:45.237 --> 00:07:49.992
need to be built so that they're testable
and robust before they're released out

00:07:50.006 --> 00:07:52.151
to developers throughout your teams.

00:07:53.096 --> 00:07:55.904
But they can also be used
in this joyous world of AI.

00:07:56.256 --> 00:08:00.692
You can use acceptance criteria to
push into a prompt, and then you've

00:08:00.705 --> 00:08:06.259
got very specific ways that your AI,
um, knows how to build components.

00:08:06.435 --> 00:08:08.259
So they're very, very valuable there.

00:08:09.143 --> 00:08:12.963
And, um, a lot of you probably do this
anyway, but you can add them to-- as

00:08:12.972 --> 00:08:17.807
part of your skills.md files, and they
will actually be pushed in directly,

00:08:17.991 --> 00:08:20.873
and they can be brought in and out as
needed, depending on the components,

00:08:20.879 --> 00:08:24.755
et cetera, so they can help AI build,
um, you know, whenever they're,

00:08:24.783 --> 00:08:26.053
they're building any sort of component.

00:08:27.546 --> 00:08:29.144
So that's how they can be used.

00:08:29.473 --> 00:08:32.058
Now, during this session, we're
gonna talk about two different

00:08:32.058 --> 00:08:34.126
levels of acceptance criteria.

00:08:35.063 --> 00:08:41.203
Um, and they are page and user flow level,
and the second one is component level.

00:08:41.750 --> 00:08:45.002
Now, I grew up in a world of
building components with developers

00:08:45.002 --> 00:08:46.496
and building design systems.

00:08:46.797 --> 00:08:50.874
So for most people, they think
about it as a second level down,

00:08:51.128 --> 00:08:55.240
and that is on a component level,
you know, like on a dropdown or

00:08:55.240 --> 00:08:56.902
on a form field or on a button.

00:08:57.748 --> 00:09:01.234
But often in the world that I've
done when I've gone into training

00:09:01.533 --> 00:09:04.484
is that there's that broader
level that's completely missing.

00:09:05.114 --> 00:09:08.003
So we're gonna be talking about
that higher level as well, which

00:09:08.004 --> 00:09:10.000
is page or user flow level.

00:09:10.375 --> 00:09:12.799
So you'll see throughout this thing,
we're gonna talk about those two.

00:09:12.799 --> 00:09:16.792
And in fact, we're gonna look right
through page and user flow and then down

00:09:16.792 --> 00:09:18.664
through component level ones as well.

00:09:19.809 --> 00:09:23.707
So let's start with page or user
flow level acceptance criteria.

00:09:24.395 --> 00:09:25.178
And so what are they?

00:09:25.465 --> 00:09:27.906
Well, pages-- I'm just gonna
define them in very basic.

00:09:27.906 --> 00:09:31.199
I mean, this sounds very obvious, but
a page is basically anything that's

00:09:31.205 --> 00:09:36.069
put together, um, a screen or a page
where, uh, a concept is put together,

00:09:36.338 --> 00:09:40.696
um, in a digital product, and they
often bring multiple components

00:09:40.696 --> 00:09:42.302
together into a single layout.

00:09:43.179 --> 00:09:47.524
So it could be something like a contact
form, a dashboard screen, search

00:09:47.524 --> 00:09:49.193
results screen, those sort of things.

00:09:50.016 --> 00:09:54.481
A user flow is a much broader concept,
and that's an end-to-end sequence

00:09:54.482 --> 00:09:57.309
of pages or states, depending on
whether it's a single-page app.

00:09:58.136 --> 00:10:01.893
And these, uh, guide users
through some sort of process.

00:10:02.902 --> 00:10:06.151
And each step, there might be
multiple screens along the way, or

00:10:06.151 --> 00:10:07.698
there could be just a tiny number.

00:10:08.411 --> 00:10:11.393
So a lot of people think, oh, well,
a login sc-- uh, process is just

00:10:11.395 --> 00:10:14.393
one screen, but often it's got
different states, and it might have

00:10:14.393 --> 00:10:15.788
an end screen, which is different.

00:10:16.007 --> 00:10:18.918
So a login process is
actually still a flow.

00:10:19.437 --> 00:10:20.834
The same with submitting a complaint.

00:10:20.834 --> 00:10:23.710
It looks like it's a form, but in
reality, there might be different

00:10:23.712 --> 00:10:25.400
states to it or different steps.

00:10:25.805 --> 00:10:29.308
Um, and I worked in insurance,
uh, for a long while, so getting

00:10:29.309 --> 00:10:31.527
a quote is a full process.

00:10:31.753 --> 00:10:35.574
So it might start with, do you wanna
get house insurance today, and takes

00:10:35.575 --> 00:10:39.410
you through that quote flow until
you've, you know, got a quote for

00:10:39.410 --> 00:10:41.428
insurance or you've purchased insurance.

00:10:42.062 --> 00:10:46.167
And even something like reset password, a
very simple one, that might take multiple

00:10:46.177 --> 00:10:49.444
screens or a, a full flow to get there.

00:10:51.110 --> 00:10:54.415
So what's the purpose of these
user flow, uh, uh, the page

00:10:54.418 --> 00:10:55.769
or user flow level components?

00:10:55.951 --> 00:11:00.460
Is to ensure that acce-accessibility
is considered and tested, um,

00:11:00.528 --> 00:11:03.704
up at this higher level, not
just down at a component level.

00:11:04.409 --> 00:11:09.602
And an example might be all interactions,
including navigation, opening and closing

00:11:09.602 --> 00:11:15.328
elements, entering data, and submissions
must be completed only using a keyboard.

00:11:17.214 --> 00:11:18.007
So where do they fit?

00:11:18.271 --> 00:11:20.649
Well, these ones are very broad
and high, so they fit in user

00:11:20.649 --> 00:11:22.367
stories, definition of done.

00:11:22.656 --> 00:11:24.269
Um, we talked about these before.

00:11:24.420 --> 00:11:28.366
Um, but they can also be pushed into
backlog, et cetera, to review backlog.

00:11:28.872 --> 00:11:32.710
And, um, you'll see there's a slight
difference between, uh, page user

00:11:32.711 --> 00:11:37.626
flow to component because these
are often owned by POs or BAs, uh,

00:11:37.664 --> 00:11:40.063
designers, developers, and QAs.

00:11:41.925 --> 00:11:44.116
So why do we need this higher level?

00:11:45.451 --> 00:11:49.653
Well, many teams focus on making
sure that each of the components are

00:11:49.653 --> 00:11:53.405
accessible, or they assume they're
ac-accessible coming from their

00:11:53.406 --> 00:11:54.997
design system, and that's great.

00:11:55.266 --> 00:11:57.322
Um, and we were gonna
talk about them soon.

00:11:57.741 --> 00:12:03.218
But there's also critical accessibility
that happens outside of your components.

00:12:03.579 --> 00:12:07.669
And working design systems for years,
the analogy I used to use, regardless of

00:12:07.669 --> 00:12:13.095
accessibility, is our job was to build the
most beautiful, robust building blocks.

00:12:13.598 --> 00:12:18.055
But teams could still put them together
poorly, and the analogy might be

00:12:18.058 --> 00:12:19.822
that you've got Lego building blocks.

00:12:20.169 --> 00:12:23.377
And as you know, each Lego building
block is beautifully robust.

00:12:23.568 --> 00:12:24.574
It's consistent.

00:12:24.920 --> 00:12:27.905
But you could build a brick
wall with Lego that's beautiful

00:12:27.905 --> 00:12:29.278
and robust and overlapping.

00:12:29.525 --> 00:12:31.918
You could build a brick wall,
which is terrible and, you know,

00:12:31.920 --> 00:12:33.412
you push it over and it collapses.

00:12:33.602 --> 00:12:37.156
So even with the best beautiful
building blocks, there's gonna

00:12:37.156 --> 00:12:40.838
be issues at a higher level,
and the same with accessibility.

00:12:42.122 --> 00:12:45.386
So, um, it's-- it-- because-- and
the main reason is because you're

00:12:45.386 --> 00:12:47.056
dealing with the product as a whole

00:12:50.125 --> 00:12:55.242
So let's talk about some common issues the
first one that only ah appears sort of at

00:12:55.242 --> 00:13:00.385
this high level is poor heading hierarchy
and you only notice that when you put

00:13:00.385 --> 00:13:02.180
a page together or a flow together.

00:13:02.358 --> 00:13:05.792
You wanna make sure that the headings
are meaningful and follow a logical

00:13:05.797 --> 00:13:11.537
order focus management is the most
important thing that designers and

00:13:11.538 --> 00:13:14.905
developers forget, and it might
be something like when a modal

00:13:14.906 --> 00:13:17.042
fires, where do you send focus?

00:13:17.365 --> 00:13:21.327
When a user travels through a form
and hit submit, where does focus go?

00:13:21.604 --> 00:13:23.985
Uh, does it go to a, um, a form field?

00:13:24.161 --> 00:13:26.719
Does it go to a message above
that helps people understand it?

00:13:26.997 --> 00:13:30.587
Does it sit down on the button and
not do anything and confuse users?

00:13:31.727 --> 00:13:33.194
Um, incorrect focus order.

00:13:33.393 --> 00:13:36.718
So does the page move around
and jar in a different way?

00:13:36.955 --> 00:13:41.358
So some keyboard users need to know
exactly where that page is and what's

00:13:41.359 --> 00:13:45.471
going on and don't want focus to
suddenly jump across to the other end

00:13:45.472 --> 00:13:47.447
of the page where they might lose it.

00:13:48.729 --> 00:13:50.301
Inconsistent navigation patterns.

00:13:50.323 --> 00:13:52.988
These are things that only
exist up at that higher level.

00:13:53.800 --> 00:13:56.555
Uh, insufficient feedback and
instructions, things like that.

00:13:56.752 --> 00:14:00.187
So for example, when user
submits a form, are there errors?

00:14:00.222 --> 00:14:01.579
Are they clearly defined?

00:14:01.901 --> 00:14:04.238
And again, when people are
focusing on small components,

00:14:04.238 --> 00:14:05.482
they sometimes miss these.

00:14:06.540 --> 00:14:08.286
Missing or unclear page titles.

00:14:08.286 --> 00:14:10.921
This is a really important
thing for single-page apps.

00:14:11.281 --> 00:14:14.535
A lot of developers build beautiful
single-page apps, but they forget that the

00:14:14.535 --> 00:14:20.092
document title can and should change as
you're moving through these pseudo-pages.

00:14:20.378 --> 00:14:20.870
Why?

00:14:21.108 --> 00:14:24.217
Because some screen readers get lost,
and one of the most common things they'll

00:14:24.217 --> 00:14:29.098
do is just use a command to hear the
page title to understand where they are.

00:14:30.223 --> 00:14:32.117
Keyboard traps and things like that.

00:14:32.310 --> 00:14:35.370
Now, they exist at a component
level, but they also exist when

00:14:35.370 --> 00:14:37.045
you put a whole page together.

00:14:38.045 --> 00:14:40.341
And then there's just broader
things like cognitive load.

00:14:40.341 --> 00:14:41.955
Is the page too complicated?

00:14:42.536 --> 00:14:43.670
Uh, timeouts.

00:14:43.699 --> 00:14:46.670
Again, timeouts very rarely
exist at a component level,

00:14:46.944 --> 00:14:48.550
but they exist at a page level.

00:14:49.469 --> 00:14:53.454
So all these types of things, in
almost every case, some keyboard traps

00:14:53.459 --> 00:14:57.266
could exist down at the component
level, but almost all these only exist

00:14:57.426 --> 00:15:00.086
up at that page or user flow level.

00:15:01.453 --> 00:15:04.435
And that's why we have to write
this level com- of compliance.

00:15:05.176 --> 00:15:09.690
So let's now zoom down to the world
that I grew up in, which is testing, um,

00:15:09.786 --> 00:15:11.663
and building tight little components.

00:15:12.161 --> 00:15:15.295
So what for these are,
is for UI components.

00:15:15.529 --> 00:15:18.179
And if you've ever heard the term,
hopefully you have, but they're

00:15:18.236 --> 00:15:23.732
distinct, um, interface elements that
are used to do a specific function.

00:15:24.476 --> 00:15:27.388
And like we talked about
before, like Lego, they're the

00:15:27.401 --> 00:15:29.201
building blocks of websites.

00:15:30.111 --> 00:15:31.892
And they could be tiny ones.

00:15:31.930 --> 00:15:34.022
Like they're-- to me,
they're all components.

00:15:34.332 --> 00:15:37.194
I don't know if you grew up in the
world of Atomic where we had, you know,

00:15:37.531 --> 00:15:38.973
um, all these different variations.

00:15:39.144 --> 00:15:42.280
But broadly speaking, there are
just simple ones and complex ones.

00:15:42.281 --> 00:15:44.010
But they're still basically components.

00:15:44.230 --> 00:15:49.680
So it could be tiny things like a form
field or a label or a button, but it

00:15:49.683 --> 00:15:55.329
could be broader, complex things like
autocomplete dropdowns, modals, date

00:15:55.332 --> 00:15:57.155
pickers, in-page tabs, et cetera.

00:15:58.576 --> 00:16:02.259
So what's the purpose of
component-level acceptance criteria?

00:16:02.579 --> 00:16:07.191
It's just to make sure that any component
that's used by a team or by multiple

00:16:07.191 --> 00:16:10.391
teams, um, is accessible and compliant.

00:16:11.372 --> 00:16:14.253
So it could be something like
the dropdown can easily be

00:16:14.254 --> 00:16:16.036
opened using keyboard controls.

00:16:16.289 --> 00:16:18.651
And that should be something that a
tester can quickly go and check and

00:16:18.651 --> 00:16:25.026
go, "Yes, it can. No, it can't." So
the owners here are slightly different.

00:16:25.478 --> 00:16:30.122
Notice before, for the page and,
and c- uh, user flow level, it was

00:16:30.131 --> 00:16:32.247
POs and BAs were the primary owner.

00:16:32.646 --> 00:16:36.596
But down at this level, it's design
system leads are the one that should

00:16:36.608 --> 00:16:38.153
be building and writing these.

00:16:38.557 --> 00:16:42.050
And then they're useful for designers,
developers, and testers again.

00:16:44.345 --> 00:16:48.435
So let's now jump into the, the
purpose of this is how do we write

00:16:48.654 --> 00:16:53.609
these, um, component level, uh,
d- uh, um, page level versions?

00:16:54.306 --> 00:16:57.500
So there are many ways you
could write acceptance criteria.

00:16:57.675 --> 00:17:00.795
And by the way, this model that
I'm gonna teach you can be used for

00:17:00.819 --> 00:17:02.555
any type of acceptance criteria.

00:17:02.733 --> 00:17:05.450
You could probably take out a few
steps, but the rigor that we're

00:17:05.453 --> 00:17:07.235
talking about could be useful anywhere.

00:17:08.383 --> 00:17:11.540
I'm gonna show you some steps, but I
should warn you, you're gonna be shocked

00:17:11.561 --> 00:17:15.602
by how many steps there are on the next
screen, uh, because there's quite a lot.

00:17:16.456 --> 00:17:18.349
So there's actually eight steps here.

00:17:18.790 --> 00:17:23.174
Um, and so we're gonna start with
you choose a thing you wanna review.

00:17:23.351 --> 00:17:27.040
Now, if you're in a sprint cycle, you
already know what it is, but it might be

00:17:27.257 --> 00:17:29.509
a, a login screen or something like that.

00:17:29.835 --> 00:17:34.168
Um, so you need to work out what the thing
is that you're going to be reviewing,

00:17:34.739 --> 00:17:36.687
and then you pick a type of people.

00:17:36.858 --> 00:17:41.214
Now, we're gonna have about six different
broad groups, and so you choose one

00:17:41.215 --> 00:17:42.947
of those groups to roll through first.

00:17:43.143 --> 00:17:47.281
And you'll see by the time we get to step
eight, we go back through each group, but

00:17:47.281 --> 00:17:51.196
we do one group of people at a time, and
I'll explain why we do that in a second.

00:17:51.764 --> 00:17:55.850
Then we think about how that group
of people might be interacting

00:17:55.892 --> 00:17:57.447
with this thing you're building.

00:17:57.897 --> 00:17:59.046
Are they using a screen reader?

00:17:59.076 --> 00:18:00.127
Are they using keyboard?

00:18:00.331 --> 00:18:01.792
Are they using voice control?

00:18:02.094 --> 00:18:04.359
Um, are they using any other mechanism?

00:18:04.546 --> 00:18:06.634
Are they blowing the screen up
so they could see it because

00:18:06.635 --> 00:18:07.578
they've got low vision?

00:18:07.971 --> 00:18:11.493
There are multiple ways that
they might be using technology or

00:18:11.493 --> 00:18:13.596
settings to interact with your page.

00:18:14.065 --> 00:18:16.821
And from that we can list
barriers that they might face.

00:18:17.059 --> 00:18:22.059
This group of people using this technology
might face these types of barriers.

00:18:22.674 --> 00:18:24.234
Then we do something odd.

00:18:24.540 --> 00:18:29.568
We turn them into test questions
And from the questions we

00:18:29.568 --> 00:18:31.180
write acceptance criteria.

00:18:31.400 --> 00:18:33.243
It sounds like a long, long way around it.

00:18:33.558 --> 00:18:38.050
And then we just add some simple tests so
that when we hand them over to QAs, they

00:18:38.051 --> 00:18:42.185
already have the acceptance criteria,
a question, which is the opposite

00:18:42.186 --> 00:18:45.714
of the test criteria, and some steps
that, so that they know how to test it.

00:18:46.264 --> 00:18:50.042
And then we just repeat that
through each group, u- user group.

00:18:50.289 --> 00:18:52.985
And the reason is so we can
focus on one group at a time.

00:18:53.454 --> 00:18:56.223
Now, some of you might be saying,
"What the hell? Why are there so many

00:18:56.223 --> 00:19:01.100
steps?" Well, as you get confident, you
can move through these very quickly.

00:19:01.317 --> 00:19:05.786
But when you're first doing this, it, it's
a really good exercise to build them out.

00:19:06.014 --> 00:19:07.514
So I run this as a workshop.

00:19:07.575 --> 00:19:11.649
Um, it's a big workshop that we run
for teams around the world, and we

00:19:11.649 --> 00:19:16.639
do this, um, as big activities where
either in person or on whiteboards

00:19:16.644 --> 00:19:21.447
or on, uh, Figma boards, et cetera,
people, um, build out these activities.

00:19:21.628 --> 00:19:25.560
And at first it's hard, it's flexing
a muscle, but as you get better at

00:19:25.561 --> 00:19:27.327
it, um, you can skip over steps.

00:19:27.328 --> 00:19:30.250
And so, um, you can either
combine steps or skip over them

00:19:30.256 --> 00:19:31.840
because you know what's going on.

00:19:32.128 --> 00:19:34.966
But when you're first, first doing
it, it's really important to build

00:19:34.966 --> 00:19:36.385
them out as a series of steps.

00:19:37.247 --> 00:19:41.418
The most important reason, though, it
helps make sure that we're focusing

00:19:41.418 --> 00:19:45.629
on different types of users as we
go through, and that, so that w-

00:19:45.651 --> 00:19:47.242
we don't forget any type of group.

00:19:48.361 --> 00:19:53.270
So the other thing is that I put testing
questions before acceptance criteria.

00:19:53.420 --> 00:19:54.344
So why do that?

00:19:54.822 --> 00:19:59.158
Well, I found after a long time
of teaching this, that it was

00:19:59.223 --> 00:20:02.748
easier to people think about
a question than a statement.

00:20:02.931 --> 00:20:06.558
Now, I know that sounds odd, but
naturally when we're thinking through

00:20:06.559 --> 00:20:10.966
this process, we would do something
like, "Can that user do this?"

00:20:11.400 --> 00:20:15.522
So it's actually easier to frame
it as a question to start with, and

00:20:15.524 --> 00:20:19.149
then once you've written all the
questions, just reverse them into

00:20:19.149 --> 00:20:21.347
acceptance criteria statements.

00:20:21.524 --> 00:20:22.322
It's more natural.

00:20:23.454 --> 00:20:27.012
It also adds rigor, 'cause as
we'll see, we need both versions.

00:20:27.012 --> 00:20:31.195
We need the acceptance criteria
and as a question so that the,

00:20:31.241 --> 00:20:33.841
uh, QAs can test against it.

00:20:35.032 --> 00:20:36.806
Uh, and yeah, it supports QAs.

00:20:37.351 --> 00:20:40.458
So let's go through this, and
we'll take a look as we go.

00:20:40.822 --> 00:20:43.334
So the first one is we're gonna
ch- uh, choose your component.

00:20:43.571 --> 00:20:46.031
Uh, could be a contact form,
dashboard screen, et cetera.

00:20:46.242 --> 00:20:47.712
All these things we talked about.

00:20:47.984 --> 00:20:52.428
Now for our activity, I'm just
gonna show you, we're going to

00:20:52.569 --> 00:20:54.940
just use a simple contact form.

00:20:55.364 --> 00:20:59.619
And as we travel through, you
can see it's got different states

00:21:04.670 --> 00:21:06.802
Now this will be available
in slides as you go through.

00:21:07.159 --> 00:21:07.991
So that's our thing.

00:21:08.161 --> 00:21:09.773
Now we've got to pick a group of people.

00:21:10.375 --> 00:21:13.212
So there are a bunch of different
groups of people that we

00:21:13.212 --> 00:21:14.825
bundle together into groups.

00:21:15.157 --> 00:21:19.011
And so you can choose, uh, and
obviously all these people need to be

00:21:19.013 --> 00:21:20.830
checked, but let's go through them.

00:21:20.969 --> 00:21:25.205
There's people with no vision, people
with reduced vision, people with reduced

00:21:25.225 --> 00:21:29.761
color vision, sometimes called color
blindness, people with reduced movement,

00:21:30.305 --> 00:21:34.896
people with varied learning or cognition,
and people with reduced or no hearing.

00:21:35.068 --> 00:21:38.479
And in some cases, people can
take out the last group if there

00:21:38.480 --> 00:21:41.458
are no, um, audio or video files.

00:21:41.675 --> 00:21:45.383
I still think it's useful to include
them anyway, but, um, that's up to you.

00:21:46.235 --> 00:21:48.316
Why focus on these
groups of people though?

00:21:48.898 --> 00:21:52.165
Because what it means is that we
don't leave groups of people out.

00:21:52.540 --> 00:21:56.322
A lot of times when I work with
teams, they focus on screen

00:21:56.322 --> 00:21:57.725
reader users, which is great.

00:21:57.867 --> 00:21:58.951
They're an important user.

00:21:59.205 --> 00:22:01.587
But all these other groups
of people get forgotten.

00:22:01.800 --> 00:22:06.673
So it means that all groups are,
are included and no group is

00:22:06.681 --> 00:22:10.804
forgotten, especially people with
varied learning or cognition.

00:22:12.573 --> 00:22:14.980
It also means that it's
not as overwhelming.

00:22:15.410 --> 00:22:19.403
Thinking about accessibility is very
overwhelming for, you know, when you're

00:22:19.406 --> 00:22:21.083
looking at a page or a user flow.

00:22:21.507 --> 00:22:24.766
And what it means is we're just
talking about a subset of people on

00:22:24.780 --> 00:22:30.121
this thing, this page, this contact
form, this group of people, how are

00:22:30.136 --> 00:22:31.864
they gonna have run into problems?

00:22:33.176 --> 00:22:34.868
Um, it also shifts the mindset.

00:22:35.371 --> 00:22:38.319
I actually don't really
care about WCAG compliance.

00:22:38.321 --> 00:22:41.788
I care about people, and WCAG
is there just to help people.

00:22:41.939 --> 00:22:45.408
So it actually is a better framing
for teams because you're thinking

00:22:45.409 --> 00:22:47.791
about people, not just compliance.

00:22:49.050 --> 00:22:51.940
And there's something really important,
which is the order we write them.

00:22:52.225 --> 00:22:53.769
The order's really important.

00:22:54.249 --> 00:22:58.319
So ideally, we wanna focus on
appearance first, and then work

00:22:58.320 --> 00:23:00.510
down to deep technology last.

00:23:01.183 --> 00:23:04.590
So the order that I run them
through is I start with people

00:23:04.600 --> 00:23:06.950
with varied learning or cognition.

00:23:07.306 --> 00:23:12.180
So these could be people with, uh,
ADHD, autism, dyslexia, also learning

00:23:12.181 --> 00:23:17.398
conditions, English as a second language,
people, um, with learning disabilities and

00:23:17.398 --> 00:23:21.461
all sorts of other things, uh, including,
um, uh, short-term memory issues.

00:23:21.793 --> 00:23:25.964
All these broad groups of people need the
same sort of thing, which is consistency,

00:23:25.971 --> 00:23:28.293
clarity, plain language, et cetera.

00:23:28.451 --> 00:23:30.478
So they're a good group
of people to start with.

00:23:30.822 --> 00:23:31.258
Why?

00:23:31.663 --> 00:23:35.538
Because if you build a component and
it doesn't work f- work for these

00:23:35.540 --> 00:23:39.728
group of people, it's gonna fail
for everyone because they're gonna

00:23:39.760 --> 00:23:42.102
bring up three types of issues.

00:23:42.588 --> 00:23:45.254
It's something to do with the content,
which means it's not understandable.

00:23:45.437 --> 00:23:49.981
It's something that means it's not
clear, or it's a fundamental UX problem.

00:23:50.422 --> 00:23:53.672
And if it fails here,
you've wasted your time.

00:23:53.990 --> 00:23:57.977
Often what I've done is people have asked
me to bring in people with disabilities,

00:23:58.253 --> 00:24:03.607
like screen reader users, and test on
a component, but it fails basic UX.

00:24:03.879 --> 00:24:07.904
And so we've wasted everyone's
time because it, it didn't even

00:24:07.907 --> 00:24:10.638
work from a UX perspective, let
alone a screen reader perspective.

00:24:10.875 --> 00:24:15.198
So doing this group of people first
is really valuable And they resolve

00:24:15.198 --> 00:24:16.550
big issues before we move on.

00:24:16.851 --> 00:24:20.425
And then we work with people with
reduced color vision and reduced

00:24:20.425 --> 00:24:24.432
vision because we're talking about
the exterior design of the concept.

00:24:24.634 --> 00:24:26.505
Can it be, uh, enlarged?

00:24:26.506 --> 00:24:28.303
Does it have good color
contrast, et cetera?

00:24:29.041 --> 00:24:32.308
And these help you understand
i- does it have good contrast?

00:24:32.389 --> 00:24:33.434
Is the layout clear?

00:24:33.697 --> 00:24:37.050
What happens when it zooms, et
cetera, or it gets inverted?

00:24:37.972 --> 00:24:41.457
And then finally, we focus on the
technology ones, which are people

00:24:41.457 --> 00:24:45.155
with reduced movement, and the very
last group is people with no vision.

00:24:45.449 --> 00:24:50.149
So these groups help you uncover
techn- uh, techn- know- knowledge

00:24:51.304 --> 00:24:54.015
technical or structural sort of issues.

00:24:54.226 --> 00:24:58.262
So can it be operated with a
keyboard, focus order, screen reader

00:24:58.263 --> 00:25:00.029
compliance, semantics, et cetera?

00:25:00.338 --> 00:25:03.483
So you'll see that we're getting more
and more technical, and that should

00:25:03.491 --> 00:25:05.024
be the last group of people you do it.

00:25:05.976 --> 00:25:09.819
Uh, now for our example, let's pretend
we're doing that contact form we

00:25:09.821 --> 00:25:13.407
talked about, and we're gonna just
choose people with reduced movement,

00:25:13.574 --> 00:25:15.096
'cause it's a good group to focus on.

00:25:15.722 --> 00:25:19.736
And a lot of the things we need for
people with reduced movement also affect

00:25:19.753 --> 00:25:22.611
screen reader users So let's click on.

00:25:22.967 --> 00:25:25.440
So now we have our form,
and we have our group.

00:25:25.934 --> 00:25:29.014
We need to consider how that group
might interact with the page.

00:25:29.682 --> 00:25:34.953
Um, how do they… Uh, so each group is
gonna rely on different technologies.

00:25:35.366 --> 00:25:37.757
So it could be a keyboard
or keyboard equivalent.

00:25:37.965 --> 00:25:39.075
It could be a screen reader.

00:25:39.300 --> 00:25:40.679
It could be voice control.

00:25:40.720 --> 00:25:43.894
I work with people who have no
movement at all, use their voice.

00:25:44.244 --> 00:25:45.582
It could be magnification.

00:25:45.621 --> 00:25:49.838
I work with people with low vision
who have Firefox set to 500%, or

00:25:49.838 --> 00:25:53.251
they use ZoomText or something
like that to zoom in on the page.

00:25:53.642 --> 00:25:57.310
Um, user settings, they might just have
font size set to larger, et cetera.

00:25:57.735 --> 00:26:01.425
They might need captions or
simplified layouts if in case

00:26:01.425 --> 00:26:02.921
they just wanna focus on the page.

00:26:03.784 --> 00:26:07.208
So for our example, people with
reduced movement and technologies,

00:26:07.794 --> 00:26:10.148
we-it's gonna be three types of things.

00:26:10.449 --> 00:26:14.413
It's gonna be just using the keyboard,
no mouse, or the equivalent of a

00:26:14.413 --> 00:26:16.078
keyboard, a keyboard alternative.

00:26:16.432 --> 00:26:19.222
I work with people who use mouth
sticks or head pointers, where they

00:26:19.222 --> 00:26:20.776
tap the keyboard to move through.

00:26:21.146 --> 00:26:24.745
It could be sip-and-puff device,
where those sips and puffs, uh,

00:26:24.766 --> 00:26:29.960
mirror keystrokes, or even switches
on the sides of their wheelchair,

00:26:29.960 --> 00:26:34.689
et cetera, or some sort of voice
control, like Dragon or built straight

00:26:34.776 --> 00:26:37.242
into, um, operating systems now.

00:26:37.465 --> 00:26:39.925
There's a bunch of great,
uh, voice controls.

00:26:41.228 --> 00:26:41.472
Okay.

00:26:41.475 --> 00:26:44.480
The next thing is list potential
barriers they may face.

00:26:44.918 --> 00:26:49.209
So on our contact form, we can look
through that and think about how those

00:26:49.224 --> 00:26:50.950
groups of people might need to operate.

00:26:51.234 --> 00:26:55.766
The simplest thing you can do is use the
Tab key and just tab through the page.

00:26:56.144 --> 00:27:00.111
So we're looking for things like a
logical tab order, keyboard traps

00:27:00.112 --> 00:27:03.484
where they get stuck, custom controls
that aren't operated with keyboard,

00:27:03.789 --> 00:27:05.438
no visual indicators, et cetera.

00:27:05.760 --> 00:27:09.315
All these things are common, uh,
issues that that group would have.

00:27:10.170 --> 00:27:14.077
Step five, now we need to
turn them into questions.

00:27:15.009 --> 00:27:19.612
So what we need to do is write
them out as clear pass and fail

00:27:19.634 --> 00:27:21.320
outcomes, but as a question.

00:27:21.962 --> 00:27:26.307
So each test question, you should be
able to ask, uh, the question and say,

00:27:26.598 --> 00:27:29.960
"Yes, it passes," or, "No, it fails."

00:27:30.168 --> 00:27:32.195
A lot of people mix and match these.

00:27:32.492 --> 00:27:35.695
Every single thing should start
with it's gotta be yes, so you're

00:27:35.695 --> 00:27:38.757
not writing them in the negative,
'cause then everyone else has to

00:27:38.918 --> 00:27:40.436
reverse engineer them in their head.

00:27:40.765 --> 00:27:45.945
Every single question, the answer should
be, "Yes, it passes." And if it's no,

00:27:46.109 --> 00:27:48.325
that means it's a clear sign of failure.

00:27:49.455 --> 00:27:56.217
So an example might be, are instructions,
labels, and feedback easy to understand?

00:27:56.915 --> 00:28:01.687
And the answer should be, "Yes,
on this contact form, instructions

00:28:01.687 --> 00:28:06.607
and labels are easy to understand,"
or, "No, there's a problem here.

00:28:06.910 --> 00:28:12.781
Instructions and labels on this form
aren't easy understand." So tips for when

00:28:12.781 --> 00:28:19.084
writing these always phrases a question,
it must be testable, one statement only.

00:28:19.092 --> 00:28:24.139
So a lot of people go, "It's gotta do X
and Y to do this." Now, suddenly you've

00:28:24.139 --> 00:28:27.094
got two concepts that the QA has to test.

00:28:27.427 --> 00:28:28.542
But here's the problem.

00:28:28.766 --> 00:28:32.554
If you put two things in one
question, what happens if one

00:28:32.554 --> 00:28:34.033
of them passes and one fails?

00:28:34.208 --> 00:28:35.341
What does the QA do?

00:28:35.656 --> 00:28:38.244
So we want one thing per question.

00:28:38.534 --> 00:28:39.947
We never bundle them together.

00:28:40.110 --> 00:28:43.798
Even if there's more of these, we want
it so that everyone in the team can go,

00:28:44.010 --> 00:28:49.955
"Oh, we're testing this one thing, and
it passes or it fails." Uh, and we should

00:28:49.966 --> 00:28:51.850
be simple, simple and user-focused.

00:28:53.187 --> 00:28:56.258
Common mistakes are too
broad, are too vague.

00:28:56.367 --> 00:28:59.399
Everyone looks at the team and goes,
"What the hell does that mean?" Um, as

00:28:59.399 --> 00:29:03.706
we said before, multiple concepts in
a question, uh, not actually phrased

00:29:03.706 --> 00:29:05.735
as a question and not testable.

00:29:05.879 --> 00:29:11.433
If it's not testable, how is your QA gonna
say, "Yes, it's passes," or, "It's done"?

00:29:11.697 --> 00:29:14.180
So everything must be
testable at some level.

00:29:14.315 --> 00:29:17.330
It doesn't have to be machine testable,
but a human should be able to look

00:29:17.330 --> 00:29:23.413
at that and go, "Yes, it passes,"
or, or, "No, it fails." Okay, so, uh,

00:29:23.416 --> 00:29:26.128
normally I do this as a bit of activity,
but we're gonna do this on screen.

00:29:26.397 --> 00:29:29.273
So I'll just do the- these,
and we can run through them.

00:29:29.655 --> 00:29:33.810
So the first one is, what do
we do if there is a barrier

00:29:34.321 --> 00:29:36.308
called incorrect focus order?

00:29:36.947 --> 00:29:40.687
Well, the way we write that is
think about it and go, does f- uh,

00:29:40.729 --> 00:29:45.305
keyboard focus move through the page
in a logical and meaningful order?

00:29:45.556 --> 00:29:47.364
And so then we go, yes, it does.

00:29:48.017 --> 00:29:48.668
No, it doesn't.

00:29:49.873 --> 00:29:53.371
Another one is keyboard traps, meaning
the keyboard gets stuck somewhere

00:29:53.551 --> 00:29:55.554
and a keyboard user can't proceed.

00:29:56.522 --> 00:30:01.516
Can keyboard users move to
and away from all interactive

00:30:01.517 --> 00:30:03.746
elements without becoming trapped?

00:30:04.053 --> 00:30:08.962
Yes, they can move through them all, or
no, they get stuck here, so it's a fail.

00:30:09.949 --> 00:30:11.683
What about missing skip mechanisms?

00:30:11.693 --> 00:30:16.129
So this means that there's some way
to bypass over complex navigations, et

00:30:16.129 --> 00:30:18.470
cetera, and get straight to the main page.

00:30:19.101 --> 00:30:22.730
Is there a way for keyboard users
to skip readed content, uh, uh,

00:30:22.769 --> 00:30:26.121
to skip repeated content and move
directly to the main content?

00:30:26.281 --> 00:30:28.357
Yes, there is, or no, there's not.

00:30:28.999 --> 00:30:30.170
Auto focus issues.

00:30:30.384 --> 00:30:32.585
Uh, does focus remain
stable and consistent?

00:30:33.187 --> 00:30:36.036
Uh, custom controls that
are not keyboard accessible.

00:30:36.176 --> 00:30:37.865
Like you might have a
date picker in there.

00:30:38.247 --> 00:30:42.592
Um, can all custom controls like your
date picker be accessed and operated

00:30:42.592 --> 00:30:44.081
using the keyboard or voice control?

00:30:44.119 --> 00:30:45.011
Yes, it can.

00:30:45.125 --> 00:30:45.860
No, it can't.

00:30:46.446 --> 00:30:47.359
Capture barriers.

00:30:47.567 --> 00:30:49.587
Can they move through them with keyboard?

00:30:50.422 --> 00:30:51.539
Uh, timeouts.

00:30:51.911 --> 00:30:55.121
Uh, does the user have enough
time to complete that task?

00:30:56.363 --> 00:30:58.569
Um, so they're just a
bunch of different ones.

00:30:58.570 --> 00:31:00.248
I might skip over a
couple at the end there.

00:31:00.662 --> 00:31:04.793
Um, these are just a bunch of different
ways that we could do to test it.

00:31:05.716 --> 00:31:09.976
And then now what we need to do
is reframe every single one of

00:31:09.976 --> 00:31:11.569
these back at a positive statement.

00:31:11.943 --> 00:31:15.387
So we've taken our beautiful
questions, which is much easier to

00:31:15.389 --> 00:31:19.738
think about, and we've reframed them
as positive statements, and they

00:31:19.738 --> 00:31:22.389
become our final acceptance criteria.

00:31:23.291 --> 00:31:27.108
So we have at the top the question
we've just built, which is, does

00:31:27.377 --> 00:31:31.691
keyboard focus move through the page
in a logical and meaningful order?

00:31:32.281 --> 00:31:36.046
And all you need to do is look at
it and just change it slightly,

00:31:36.401 --> 00:31:37.598
and it becomes a question.

00:31:37.757 --> 00:31:40.949
So the trick is, it's
normally the first word, does.

00:31:41.431 --> 00:31:45.238
If we take out does, it moves from
being a question to a statement.

00:31:45.610 --> 00:31:50.109
So you could literally take off the
word does and put an S on moves, and

00:31:50.109 --> 00:31:55.127
we've gone from does keyboard, uh, uh,
focus move through to keyboard focus

00:31:55.128 --> 00:31:57.067
moves through, and now it's a statement.

00:31:57.262 --> 00:31:58.023
And here's the thing.

00:31:58.023 --> 00:32:00.022
We want them to be an exact match.

00:32:00.790 --> 00:32:03.717
So can keyboard users
move to and away from?

00:32:04.106 --> 00:32:04.648
Yes.

00:32:04.897 --> 00:32:06.672
Keyboard focus moves to and away from.

00:32:07.507 --> 00:32:09.203
Um, ooh, that one wasn't
quite right before.

00:32:09.421 --> 00:32:11.678
Um, is there a way for
keyboard users to skip?

00:32:12.153 --> 00:32:14.911
Yes, there is a way for
keyboard users to skip.

00:32:15.596 --> 00:32:18.236
So they much match directly
one to one, and the, one of the

00:32:18.236 --> 00:32:19.703
examples before didn't quite match.

00:32:19.941 --> 00:32:22.299
Um, use clear, uh, language.

00:32:22.541 --> 00:32:25.517
Keep ex- again, keep
to one thing per slide.

00:32:26.010 --> 00:32:28.191
Um, simple and clear.

00:32:29.271 --> 00:32:33.671
Um, and common mistakes that I see are
that it doesn't match the test questions.

00:32:33.672 --> 00:32:35.163
That's the weirdest one that I've seen.

00:32:35.481 --> 00:32:38.687
Um, uh, there's, uh, it's,
it's written in a negative way.

00:32:38.862 --> 00:32:40.932
This is a very common thing
when beginning, by the way.

00:32:41.174 --> 00:32:44.248
A lot of people I see write
them n- in a negative way.

00:32:44.525 --> 00:32:48.160
Uh, we want everything to be,
yes, it passes, but just, it's

00:32:48.161 --> 00:32:49.525
just simpler for everyone.

00:32:49.779 --> 00:32:52.694
That is the b-- whenever I run
these activities, the most common

00:32:52.696 --> 00:32:54.410
thing people have to struggle with.

00:32:54.657 --> 00:33:00.612
Um, multiple concepts in a statement are
very common, and just not outcome-based So

00:33:00.613 --> 00:33:02.740
let's go and do some activities on this.

00:33:03.340 --> 00:33:05.106
So here's our test question.

00:33:05.386 --> 00:33:09.371
Does focus remain stable and
predictable when moving, uh,

00:33:09.384 --> 00:33:11.036
while interacting with a page?

00:33:11.283 --> 00:33:12.629
And we just change it around.

00:33:12.993 --> 00:33:17.059
Yes, focus remains stable and
predictable when interacting with a page.

00:33:17.655 --> 00:33:20.585
Can all use-- all custom controls
be accessed by the keyboard?

00:33:20.797 --> 00:33:23.497
Yes, all custom controls can be used.

00:33:23.676 --> 00:33:27.532
By the way, it helps to say yes,
because that helps you understand that

00:33:27.533 --> 00:33:29.211
it should be written in the positive.

00:33:29.855 --> 00:33:34.580
Um, so adding a, a, in your head, the
word yes as you go through means that you

00:33:34.580 --> 00:33:36.359
know that's what the end goal should be.

00:33:36.956 --> 00:33:40.581
Can capture or verification steps
be completed using the keyboard?

00:33:40.858 --> 00:33:43.299
Yes, capture can be done with a keyboard.

00:33:44.154 --> 00:33:46.738
Is there enough time to
complete tasks without timeouts?

00:33:46.948 --> 00:33:48.822
Yes, there's enough time
to complete timeouts.

00:33:48.999 --> 00:33:52.744
Now, for keyboard users, that means
that they need to be able to be-- have a

00:33:52.745 --> 00:33:57.580
notification pop up, be able to navigate
to that, uh, that pop up and go, "I

00:33:57.581 --> 00:34:01.757
want to pause for more time. I want
to extend so I can get to more time."

00:34:02.077 --> 00:34:05.185
So for them, it's being able
to navigate to your messages.

00:34:06.537 --> 00:34:09.197
Is the currently focused
element clearly visible?

00:34:09.665 --> 00:34:12.161
Yes, the currently focused
element is clearly visible.

00:34:12.413 --> 00:34:13.335
This is critical.

00:34:13.375 --> 00:34:15.132
I work with a woman called Judith.

00:34:15.484 --> 00:34:18.241
She uses a head pointer, so she
taps to get through the screen.

00:34:18.571 --> 00:34:22.635
And sometimes, if the design hasn't done
a good job, she can't see what's in focus.

00:34:22.837 --> 00:34:23.752
Very important.

00:34:24.610 --> 00:34:28.487
Do all interactive, uh, elements
follow expected keyboard behaviors?

00:34:28.572 --> 00:34:29.443
Yes, they follow.

00:34:30.714 --> 00:34:34.991
So now that we've got them in place we're
gonna do something a little bit extra.

00:34:35.207 --> 00:34:37.424
Now, you don't have to do
this step, but this helps

00:34:37.435 --> 00:34:39.630
everyone, and it helps your QAs.

00:34:40.016 --> 00:34:44.925
Um, so we have our test criteria
and our acceptance criteria, the

00:34:44.926 --> 00:34:46.521
test questions, acceptance criteria.

00:34:46.766 --> 00:34:51.382
We actually reverse the order now, and
the final step is just to write some

00:34:51.393 --> 00:34:53.220
simple ways that people could test them.

00:34:53.506 --> 00:34:56.469
So we just write some very simple ways.

00:34:56.621 --> 00:34:58.463
Now I'm gonna just do
one here to show you.

00:34:58.991 --> 00:35:00.995
Acceptance criteria, so we flip the order.

00:35:01.314 --> 00:35:04.681
Keyboard focus moves through the page
in a logical and meaningful order,

00:35:04.987 --> 00:35:06.474
and here's our original test question.

00:35:06.714 --> 00:35:08.870
Does keyboard mo-focus
move through the page?

00:35:08.872 --> 00:35:09.947
That's for the QA.

00:35:10.212 --> 00:35:12.827
And then we, we write how do they test it.

00:35:13.099 --> 00:35:18.110
Well, you could do something simple like
use a keyboard, use Tab to move through

00:35:18.110 --> 00:35:22.696
the page, um, use Shift+Tab to move
backwards, and check that focus moves

00:35:22.697 --> 00:35:24.652
through the page in a logical order.

00:35:25.203 --> 00:35:27.889
That's all we need to do, and we just
need to write them for each of them.

00:35:28.140 --> 00:35:30.421
And now what you actually have,
I'm gonna skip over a bunch of

00:35:30.435 --> 00:35:32.422
these, they're just examples, um,

00:35:34.510 --> 00:35:37.185
um, ooh, sorry.

00:35:37.446 --> 00:35:43.131
Um, once we've done that, we now
have, um, all of our seven steps done.

00:35:43.504 --> 00:35:45.746
We have, uh, talked about the page.

00:35:45.977 --> 00:35:48.134
We've talked about the type of
user we're gonna roll through.

00:35:48.397 --> 00:35:49.899
We've talked about their barriers.

00:35:50.158 --> 00:35:52.632
We've talked about also their
technologies and the barriers they

00:35:52.633 --> 00:35:54.171
may face with their technologies.

00:35:54.585 --> 00:35:59.043
And we've talked about how, uh,
their, the, the questions and then the

00:35:59.045 --> 00:36:01.382
statements, and finally how to test them.

00:36:01.584 --> 00:36:06.297
And so eight is just going back,
uh, uh, through all that again.

00:36:06.567 --> 00:36:10.007
So we've got acceptance crit-criteria,
test questions, and simple tests,

00:36:10.276 --> 00:36:14.254
and now we just go back and do step
seven, two to seven again as we go.

00:36:15.558 --> 00:36:19.471
So, we just move through, consider how
this new group works through it, think

00:36:19.481 --> 00:36:23.942
about their barriers, test questions
just for them, statements just for them,

00:36:24.365 --> 00:36:26.076
and simple test steps just for them.

00:36:26.499 --> 00:36:26.918
Excuse me.

00:36:27.770 --> 00:36:31.976
So that's just it, and it sounds really
cumbersome when you first begin it,

00:36:32.241 --> 00:36:35.013
but as you get better at this, you can
move through them much more quickly.

00:36:35.223 --> 00:36:38.555
And when I run this as activities,
by the time we run through the first

00:36:38.555 --> 00:36:41.782
group of people, everyone's really
stuck and they, they find it hard.

00:36:42.052 --> 00:36:45.700
But by the t-time we go from round
again, people are often very good at it.

00:36:46.420 --> 00:36:50.310
Okay, so there's a criteria library
here, which I'll put in chat, and

00:36:50.311 --> 00:36:52.717
that's a page where you can go
and see a bunch of these ready.

00:36:53.201 --> 00:36:55.883
Um, but I'm just gonna jump
onto our last point here, which

00:36:55.884 --> 00:36:58.161
is what about component level?

00:36:58.720 --> 00:37:01.593
And in reality, um… Oh,
let me just jump over that.

00:37:01.837 --> 00:37:02.981
Uh, is it the same steps?

00:37:03.004 --> 00:37:03.440
Yes.

00:37:03.644 --> 00:37:07.192
We're gonna go through exactly the
same steps as last time, but we're

00:37:07.192 --> 00:37:10.288
gonna look quickly at the steps
because there's a couple of changes.

00:37:10.572 --> 00:37:13.638
So for the first one, we
used user flow level things.

00:37:13.916 --> 00:37:15.848
We just now use component level things.

00:37:16.500 --> 00:37:18.796
For step two, same as before.

00:37:18.839 --> 00:37:20.633
Go through the, the
different groups of people.

00:37:21.625 --> 00:37:23.296
Um, consider how they interact with it.

00:37:23.441 --> 00:37:27.572
Well, this is the same, but you have to be
more precise now because they are using,

00:37:27.755 --> 00:37:30.185
uh, technologies that directly impact it.

00:37:30.390 --> 00:37:36.207
So we need to look at, uh, things like
what component's expected to do, how users

00:37:36.209 --> 00:37:40.202
operate using different input devices, and
what's the expected sequence of events.

00:37:40.337 --> 00:37:42.581
So you need to know
where tabs go, et cetera.

00:37:43.737 --> 00:37:46.636
Um, and so you need to know a
little bit more about how the

00:37:46.636 --> 00:37:48.307
users travel through components.

00:37:48.624 --> 00:37:52.134
Then we need to list barriers,
and it's sort of similar for

00:37:52.136 --> 00:37:53.553
component level as page level.

00:37:53.895 --> 00:37:55.874
Um, and we'll just skip
over some of these.

00:37:55.874 --> 00:37:57.473
There are a bunch of different things.

00:37:57.567 --> 00:37:58.946
It's all in slides if you need it.

00:37:59.163 --> 00:38:00.770
But here's the ones we
need to worry about.

00:38:01.112 --> 00:38:03.840
Components are reachable,
focus order, a lot of these

00:38:03.842 --> 00:38:05.207
things we talked about before.

00:38:06.220 --> 00:38:10.506
So then we turn them into test questions,
and they are sort of the same as before.

00:38:10.992 --> 00:38:15.103
Each one needs to be a pass or a fail,
but there's a bit of additional rigor

00:38:15.103 --> 00:38:19.400
we need because for most of them,
we need to be far more rigorous at

00:38:19.400 --> 00:38:21.563
a component level than a page level.

00:38:22.295 --> 00:38:26.884
And the reason is they have clear
objectives, uh, color contrast,

00:38:26.884 --> 00:38:31.600
keyboard, ARIA roles, announcements,
so they're much, much more specific.

00:38:31.933 --> 00:38:35.636
So you should be explicit and
measurable in the way you test these.

00:38:36.145 --> 00:38:38.980
Now, the only group where it doesn't
count is for people with varied

00:38:38.981 --> 00:38:42.490
learning or cognition, 'cause they're
a little bit more vague, and you can't

00:38:42.490 --> 00:38:44.241
write them in the same precise way.

00:38:45.605 --> 00:38:48.841
Um, and once you've done that, uh,
six, seven, and eight remains the

00:38:48.841 --> 00:38:54.928
same as well So basically, um, to
wrap up, um, hopefully when you do

00:38:54.929 --> 00:38:57.615
this and you-- if you do activities
with your team, you'll learn how to

00:38:57.615 --> 00:39:01.190
apply this, this process as you go
through with the six groups of people.

00:39:01.449 --> 00:39:04.087
Um, and then, uh, what can you do next?

00:39:04.087 --> 00:39:05.796
Apply this out in the real world.

00:39:06.207 --> 00:39:07.542
So hopefully this is useful.

00:39:07.864 --> 00:39:11.034
It's just a what looks like a
very cumbersome process when you

00:39:11.035 --> 00:39:14.738
first begin it, but it adds rigor,
and as you get better at it, you

00:39:14.741 --> 00:39:16.103
move through it very quickly.

00:39:16.518 --> 00:39:18.085
As I said, I run this as a big workshop.

00:39:18.094 --> 00:39:21.265
By the time people get to the end of the
workshop, they're just zooming through it.

00:39:21.496 --> 00:39:26.132
And I've had POs and QAs say, "This
is really valuable, much more than

00:39:26.132 --> 00:39:29.892
just for accessibility, but just
writing robust acceptance criteria

00:39:29.899 --> 00:39:33.118
for any team." So that's it for me.

00:39:33.299 --> 00:39:36.624
Thank you very much for listening,
and I'll be available for Q&A.

00:39:44.830 --> 00:39:45.368
All right.

00:39:45.413 --> 00:39:45.926
Awesome.

00:39:46.303 --> 00:39:49.107
Thank you so much, Russ, uh,
for presenting and sharing your

00:39:49.107 --> 00:39:52.210
accessibility expertise with the
WordPress community and all the

00:39:52.212 --> 00:39:53.827
attendees that are gathered here today.

00:39:54.308 --> 00:39:58.529
Uh, I don't see anything in the Q&A
chat right now, so I'm gonna plug

00:39:58.536 --> 00:40:00.502
that and then we'll come back to it.

00:40:00.546 --> 00:40:04.571
If anyone has any questions, feel
free to go to the more dots if it's

00:40:04.573 --> 00:40:06.312
in there, and go to the Q&A section.

00:40:06.326 --> 00:40:09.031
It- I'm not sure where it'll,
it'll be on your specific Zoom.

00:40:09.424 --> 00:40:11.654
But in the meantime, I saw
some reactions come in.

00:40:11.922 --> 00:40:16.788
Everyone feel free to add reactions
to, to thank Russ for their time.

00:40:17.106 --> 00:40:19.968
And yeah, if it's okay with you,
Russ, I've got one question.

00:40:20.548 --> 00:40:20.558
Mm-hmm.

00:40:20.558 --> 00:40:21.464
Uh, and then we'll go from there.

00:40:22.850 --> 00:40:23.479
All right.

00:40:23.897 --> 00:40:26.989
So near the end of your
presentation, you covered converting

00:40:26.990 --> 00:40:30.982
those positive statements, um,
into test questions instead.

00:40:31.361 --> 00:40:35.754
So I noticed that sort of leaves you with
a question acceptance criteria pair for,

00:40:35.881 --> 00:40:40.261
for each piece, um, where I think you
n- mentioned that like the, the question

00:40:40.263 --> 00:40:44.768
piece is really good for QA, and then
the AC piece is good for teams that are

00:40:44.768 --> 00:40:46.354
actually going out and building products.

00:40:46.705 --> 00:40:50.752
Uh, so the question I had of that as
someone who is in the business of building

00:40:50.777 --> 00:40:54.819
things like design systems documentation
is, which is better to record?

00:40:54.915 --> 00:40:56.749
Do you record the question?

00:40:57.147 --> 00:40:59.522
Um, do you record the
acceptance criterion?

00:40:59.567 --> 00:41:01.113
Or do you record both?

00:41:01.866 --> 00:41:02.825
It's great thing, isn't it?

00:41:03.254 --> 00:41:04.988
I think it depends where
it's happening, isn't it?

00:41:05.008 --> 00:41:08.993
Because in definition of done, it's
the acceptance criteria itself.

00:41:09.498 --> 00:41:13.232
Uh, when, when QAs are reporting
it, it would be the pass

00:41:13.234 --> 00:41:15.240
or fail of the actual test.

00:41:15.490 --> 00:41:16.033
Does it make sense?

00:41:16.033 --> 00:41:18.727
Like it depends on who's doing
it and where it's happening.

00:41:18.973 --> 00:41:21.970
'Cause a lot of my work, I work in design
systems as well, and that's where I

00:41:21.970 --> 00:41:27.069
started it, trying to actually pr- provide
robust answers to people that all these

00:41:27.071 --> 00:41:28.805
components are completely accessible.

00:41:29.047 --> 00:41:33.665
So we had to have frameworks to show we'd
tested them fully, and you want to be able

00:41:33.665 --> 00:41:35.815
to say, "Yes, it passes all those things."

00:41:36.044 --> 00:41:40.047
So for the, the end output, it, it's
not only is the acceptance criteria

00:41:40.558 --> 00:41:45.147
a, a way to determine how it pa-
pass or fails, but the actual test

00:41:45.149 --> 00:41:47.582
themselves are the end, uh, result.

00:41:47.587 --> 00:41:49.696
So a pass or a fail is the old one thing.

00:41:50.138 --> 00:41:50.973
Does that answer your question?

00:41:52.612 --> 00:41:53.739
Uh, I don't know if it did.

00:41:53.749 --> 00:41:55.249
But I, I think it said basically…

00:41:56.672 --> 00:42:00.913
What I took away is, uh, documenting
it, and especially the processes on

00:42:00.913 --> 00:42:04.683
a design systems side, any amount of
information you can give people is good.

00:42:04.797 --> 00:42:07.006
Uh, and then it's a different
consideration when it comes

00:42:07.006 --> 00:42:10.252
to teaching people, whether
they're QA or product, sort of.

00:42:10.264 --> 00:42:10.524
Yeah.

00:42:10.527 --> 00:42:11.369
That's sort of what I took away.

00:42:11.555 --> 00:42:11.838
Yeah.

00:42:12.132 --> 00:42:13.012
Apologies for that.

00:42:13.988 --> 00:42:14.960
No, no, that's good.

00:42:14.977 --> 00:42:18.694
Answering live is one of the
most exciting things to do.

00:42:19.167 --> 00:42:21.518
Uh, and also asking live, 'cause
I've run out of questions.

00:42:21.544 --> 00:42:23.870
Um, was there anything that you
wanted to add from your side?

00:42:23.882 --> 00:42:28.068
Any extra context for people, um, who
are in the room today, um, or anything

00:42:28.069 --> 00:42:32.571
specific to, like, your experience with
WordPress when it comes to, um, acceptance

00:42:32.571 --> 00:42:34.103
criteria, testing, anything like that?

00:42:34.798 --> 00:42:37.592
Yeah, I used to work in Wor- WordPress
for many, many years, but I've mo-

00:42:37.657 --> 00:42:40.952
I've sort of moved away from it
recently, so I'm less in that space.

00:42:41.239 --> 00:42:43.452
But to me, I was always agnostic.

00:42:43.673 --> 00:42:45.441
I mean, WordPress was a great tool.

00:42:45.441 --> 00:42:46.937
I used it for a lot of
my sites for many years.

00:42:47.238 --> 00:42:50.898
But I was always agnostic, 'cause
when I train, I come into teams using

00:42:50.965 --> 00:42:55.431
any sort of system, so I've tried to
make that work in any environment.

00:42:55.723 --> 00:43:00.325
And even within Scrums, um, I found
that people would, uh, produce sprint

00:43:00.325 --> 00:43:04.433
cycles in different ways, so I try
not to make it sort of WordPress or

00:43:04.433 --> 00:43:09.690
React, uh, direct, or even specific
to a way a sprint might work.

00:43:10.014 --> 00:43:12.300
Um, I don't… You've probably
noticed this as well, but have you

00:43:12.301 --> 00:43:14.967
s- Have you noticed every team that
runs a sprint runs it in different

00:43:14.967 --> 00:43:16.138
ways, no matter what you say.

00:43:16.422 --> 00:43:19.652
And so, yeah, the, the way it's
built should be completely agnostic.

00:43:19.653 --> 00:43:22.735
It shouldn't… I mean, regardless,
it, it should work in React,

00:43:22.767 --> 00:43:24.268
Word- WordPress, whatever.

00:43:24.475 --> 00:43:25.188
If that makes sense.

00:43:26.419 --> 00:43:26.795
For sure.

00:43:26.824 --> 00:43:27.147
Yeah.

00:43:27.206 --> 00:43:31.686
Um, every version of Agile is in itself
agile in the way it implements Agile.

00:43:31.963 --> 00:43:35.691
Uh, and the same can be said for, um,
WordPress implementations as well.

00:43:35.697 --> 00:43:35.725
Yes.

00:43:35.847 --> 00:43:38.406
The number of different plugins
and themes and all that.

00:43:38.795 --> 00:43:41.621
Um, thank you for attending
this session with Russ.

00:43:41.665 --> 00:43:44.909
You can continue the conversation
in Slack or on social media using

00:43:44.910 --> 00:43:50.346
